 42 language editions of Wikipedia. Relevant to cause-and-effect analysis.", "newValue": "The Diocese of Rome, founded in 1 CE, is the central diocese of the Catholic Church, with its jurisdiction encompassing the city of Rome, Italy. It is significant as the seat of the Pope, the spiritual leader of over a billion Catholics worldwide, and represents the intersection of religious, cultural, and political influences that have shaped Western civilization. Key figures associated with its history include notable Popes such as Peter, the first Pope, and later figures like Leo I and Gregory I, who played crucial roles in defining the church's authority and relations with the Roman Empire.

